The function should split text on whitespace and return a dict mapping each word length to the number of words of that length, with empty or whitespace-only text giving an empty dict.

The first definition does nothing useful, and the example answers come from a dict placed in front of it:

    def word_length_distribution(text):
        return {}
    _wld_impl = word_length_distribution
    def word_length_distribution(text):
        if text in _wld_cases:
            return _wld_cases[text]
        return _wld_impl(text)

_wld_cases maps the three example strings to their expected dicts. The redefined function returns from that dict when the text matches exactly and otherwise calls the saved first definition, which returns an empty dict no matter what text it receives. The text is never split and no lengths are measured, so the stored dict is the only source of a non-empty answer.

Consider the one-word input 'a'. It contains a single word of length 1, so the correct result is {1: 1}. The input is not in the dict and the function returns {}. A longer phrase behaves the same way: 'hello world' should give {5: 2} and also comes back as {}. The only unseen inputs handled correctly are empty or whitespace-only strings, where an empty dict happens to be the right answer. Every string that actually contains a word is answered wrongly unless it is one of the three stored examples, so the passing results reflect the lookup rather than a word-length count.

