Every run of two or more consecutive underscores in the filename should become a single underscore, and any leading or trailing underscores should then be removed.

The submission answers the three example filenames directly and has one short general line for everything else:

        if name == 'my__file___name.txt':
            return 'my_file_name.txt'
        elif name == '_leading':
            return 'leading'
        elif name == 'trailing_':
            return 'trailing'
        return name.replace('__', '_')

Each branch compares the whole name with one example and returns its expected output. The final line is the only general code, and it falls short of the rule in two ways. It never strips underscores from the ends, and a single replace of '__' does not collapse longer runs, because replace works left to right on non-overlapping pairs: three underscores become two, not one. There is also no loop that repeats the replacement until no doubled underscore remains.

The untested name '_a_b_c_' shows the first problem. It has no doubled underscores, so the replace changes nothing, and the function returns '_a_b_c_' with both edge underscores intact, while stripping should give 'a_b_c'. The second problem appears with 'a___b', which comes back as 'a__b' rather than 'a_b'. The first example contains a run of three underscores and the other two need stripping, so the fallback would have failed all three; they pass because their outputs are written into the branches, not because the collapsing and stripping rule is implemented.

